# Netlify header rules for the published site. Netlify reads this file from the
# publish root; build.sh copies it into dist/ via the allowlist.
#
# ── Why the CSP is declared per page instead of once under /* ────────────────
#
# Netlify COMBINES every _headers rule whose path matches the request; a more
# specific path does not override a broader one. When the same header is set
# twice the values are both sent (Netlify may serialize them as one
# comma-separated value), and browsers treat multiple Content-Security-Policy
# policies as a set to be enforced *simultaneously* — the effective policy is
# their intersection, i.e. the most restrictive of each directive.
#
# That makes "strict under /*, relaxed for one page" impossible to express:
# /demo.html matches /* too, so a sitewide `connect-src 'self'` would intersect
# with the demo's `connect-src *` and win, blocking every visitor-supplied base
# URL — and doing it silently, which is the worst version of this bug. (An
# earlier revision of this file did exactly that; review caught it.)
#
# So the CSP is declared once per page on non-overlapping paths, and /* carries
# only the headers that are identical everywhere (duplicating those is
# harmless). Every shipped page therefore receives exactly ONE CSP.
#
# The cost is that a new page silently gets no CSP at all if someone forgets a
# rule. tests/test_website_csp_scope.py closes that: it reads the PUBLISH
# allowlist out of build.sh and fails if any shipped .html page lacks a rule, or
# if a wildcard path ever declares a CSP again.
#
# ── The policy itself ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `default-src 'self'` is what turns the site's "no external calls" claim into
# something a visitor's browser enforces rather than something we merely assert
# — no third-party script, style, font, or image origin is reachable. Two
# relaxations are deliberate:
#
#   'unsafe-inline'  Every page inlines its <style>, and the standalone demo
#                    inlines its whole engine as a single ~110KB <script>.
#                    Hashing or noncing that on a static host buys very little
#                    on a site that loads no third-party code at all.
#
#   connect-src *    ONLY on /demo.html, which drives a live assessment against
#                    whichever SparkAE instance the visitor is running. The base
#                    URL is a free-text field — commonly http://localhost:8000
#                    during an evaluation, but a remote deployment is just as
#                    legitimate — so no narrower origin list covers it without
#                    breaking real evaluations. Every other page is held to
#                    `connect-src 'self'`.
#
# Audited when this was scoped: demo.html is the only page that connects
# off-origin (10 fetches to the visitor-supplied base URL). status.html fetches
# only the relative `status-data.json`, demo-standalone.html inlines its engine
# and fetches nothing, and no other page makes a request at all — so 'self'
# costs those pages nothing.
#
# HSTS deliberately omits includeSubDomains: it would bind every current and
# future subdomain of the deployment domain (app., status., anything not
# yet stood up) to
# HTTPS-only from the first visit, which is a commitment to make on purpose
# rather than inherit from a marketing-site config.
#
# VERIFY ON DEPLOY PREVIEW after changing anything above:
#   curl -sI <preview>/demo.html | grep -i content-security-policy
#   curl -sI <preview>/          | grep -i content-security-policy
# Each must return exactly ONE policy — `connect-src *` for the demo,
# `connect-src 'self'` for everything else. Two headers, or one carrying two
# comma-separated policies, means the paths overlap again.

# Identical on every response, so /* is safe here — duplicate values of these
# headers do not intersect the way CSP does.
/*
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=(), payment=(), usb=()
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000

# ── Per-page CSP. Paths must not overlap; see the note at the top. ───────────
#
# BOTH route forms are listed for every page. Netlify serves `foo.html` at the
# extensionless `/foo` as well, and header rules match the REQUESTED path — so
# listing only `/foo.html` leaves `/foo` with no CSP at all now that `/*` no
# longer carries one (caught in review). The site's own links all use
# `.html`, but external links, search-engine canonicalisation and typed URLs
# reach the clean form.

/
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'self'; object-src 'none'

